{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T18:05:04Z","timestamp":1788285904202,"version":"build-2803163510"},"reference-count":27,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"7","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,20]],"date-time":"2009-04-20T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1240185600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Comm Pure Appl Math"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>We consider solutions to the linear wave equation \u25a1<jats:sub><jats:italic>g<\/jats:italic><\/jats:sub><jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic> = 0 on a (maximally extended) Schwarzschild spacetime with parameter <jats:italic>M<\/jats:italic> &gt; 0, evolving from sufficiently regular initial data prescribed on a complete Cauchy surface \u03a3, where the data are assumed only to decay suitably at spatial infinity. (In particular, the support of <jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic> may contain the bifurcate event horizon.) It is shown that the energy flux <jats:italic>F<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-1.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-1\"\/>(<jats:italic>\ud835\udcae<\/jats:italic>) of the solution (as measured by a strictly timelike <jats:italic>T\u0303<\/jats:italic> that asymptotically matches the static Killing field) through arbitrary achronal subsets <jats:italic>\ud835\udcae<\/jats:italic> of the black hole exterior region satisfies the bound <jats:italic>F<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-2.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-2\"\/>(<jats:italic>\ud835\udcae<\/jats:italic>) \u2264 <jats:italic>C E<\/jats:italic>(<jats:italic>v<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-3.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-3\"\/> + <jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-4.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-4\"\/>), where <jats:italic>v<\/jats:italic> and <jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic> denote the infimum of the Eddington\u2010Finkelstein advanced and retarded time of <jats:italic>\ud835\udcae<\/jats:italic>, <jats:italic>v<\/jats:italic><jats:sub>+<\/jats:sub> denotes max{1, <jats:italic>v<\/jats:italic>}, and <jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic><jats:sub>+<\/jats:sub> denotes max{1, <jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic>}, where <jats:italic>C<\/jats:italic> is a constant depending only on the parameter <jats:italic>M<\/jats:italic>, and <jats:italic>E<\/jats:italic> depends on a suitable norm of the solution on the hypersurface <jats:italic>t<\/jats:italic> \u2250 <jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic> + <jats:italic>v<\/jats:italic> = 1. (The bound applies in particular to subsets <jats:italic>\ud835\udcae<\/jats:italic> of the event horizon or null infinity.) It is also shown that <jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic> satisfies the pointwise decay estimate |<jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic>| \u2264 <jats:italic>C Ev<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-5.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-5\"\/> in the entire exterior region, and the estimates |<jats:italic>r\u03d5<\/jats:italic>| \u2264 <jats:italic>C<jats:sub>R\u0303<\/jats:sub>E<\/jats:italic>(1 + |<jats:italic>u<\/jats:italic>|)<jats:sup>\u22121\/2<\/jats:sup> and |<jats:italic>r<\/jats:italic><jats:sup>1\/2<\/jats:sup><jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic>| \u2264 <jats:italic>C<jats:sub>R\u0303<\/jats:sub>Eu<\/jats:italic><jats:inline-graphic xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" xlink:href=\"graphic\/tex2gif-stack-6.gif\" xlink:title=\"urn:x-wiley:00103640:media:CPA20281:tex2gif-stack-6\"\/> in the region {<jats:italic>r<\/jats:italic> \u2265 <jats:italic>R\u0303<\/jats:italic>} \u2229 <jats:italic>J<\/jats:italic><jats:sup>+<\/jats:sup>(\u03a3) for any <jats:italic>R\u0303<\/jats:italic> &gt; 2<jats:italic>M<\/jats:italic>. The estimates near the event horizon exploit an integral energy identity normalized to local observers. This estimate can be thought to quantify the celebrated red\u2010shift effect. The results in particular give an independent proof of the classical result |<jats:italic>\u03d5<\/jats:italic>| \u2265 <jats:italic>C E<\/jats:italic> of Kay and Wald without recourse to the discrete isometries of spacetime. \u00a9 2009 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/cpa.20281","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,20]],"date-time":"2009-04-20T17:36:53Z","timestamp":1240249013000},"page":"859-919","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":179,"title":["The red\u2010shift effect and radiation decay on black hole spacetimes"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"62","author":[{"given":"Mihalis","family":"Dafermos","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Igor","family":"Rodnianski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2009,4,20]]},"reference":[{"issue":"5","key":"e_1_2_1_2_2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"595","DOI":"10.57262\/ade\/1355926842","article-title":"Semilinear wave equations on the Schwarzschild manifold. I. Local decay estimates","volume":"8","author":"Blue P.","year":"2003","journal-title":"Adv Differential Equations"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_3_2","unstructured":"Blue P.;Soffer A.Errata for \u201cGlobal existence and scattering for the nonlinear Schrodinger equation on Schwarzschild manifolds \u201d \u201cSemilinear wave equations on the Schwarzschild manifold I: Local decay estimates \u201d and \u201cThe wave equation on the Schwarzschild metric II: Local decay for the spin 2 Regge Wheeler equation.\u201d. arXiv: gr\u2010qc\/0608073 2006."},{"key":"e_1_2_1_4_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s00220-006-0101-6"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_5_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/BF00375144"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_6_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1088\/0264-9381\/16\/12A\/302"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_7_2","series-title":"Annals of Mathematics Studies","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.1515\/9781400882687","volume-title":"The action principle and partial differential equations","author":"Christodoulou D.","year":"2000"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_8_2","series-title":"Princeton Mathematical Series","volume-title":"The global nonlinear stability of the Minkowski space","author":"Christodoulou D.","year":"1993"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_9_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1002\/cpa.20071"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_10_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s00222-005-0450-3"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_11_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.matpur.2005.04.004"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_12_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2307\/1968902"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_13_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s00220-006-1525-8"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_14_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevD.49.883"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_15_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1017\/CBO9780511524646"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_16_2","first-page":"53","volume-title":"Die Grundlagen der Physik (Zweite Mitteilung)","author":"Hilbert D.","year":"2007"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_17_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1088\/0264-9381\/4\/4\/022"},{"key":"e_1_2_1_18_2","doi-asserted-b